== Constructions on coefficient systems <sec:coefficient-constructions>

Suppose $cal(F)$ is a $G$-equivariant coefficient system on a $G$-poset or
$G$-simplicial complex $X$.

#definition[
  The *inverse limit*#idx("inverse limit")#idx("limit") (or *projective
  limit*#idx("projective", "limit")) of $cal(F)$, written $invlim cal(F)$#idx(
    "$invlim cal(F)$, $attach(limits(lim), b: arrow.r.long) cal(F)$",
  ), is a vector space $V$ together with maps $V -> cal(F)(x)$ for each
  $x in X$, commuting with restriction maps, and universal with respect to this
  property. Universal in this context means that given another $W$ and maps as
  above, there is a unique map of vector spaces $W -> V$ whose composite with
  each given map $V -> cal(F)(x)$ is the given map $W -> cal(F)(x)$.

  Dually, the *direct limit*#idx("direct limit") (also known as the *inductive
  limit*#idx("inductive limit") or *colimit*#idx("colimit") of $cal(F)$),
  written $dirlim cal(F)$, is a vector space $V$ together with maps
  $cal(F)(x) -> V$ for each $x in X$, commuting with restriction maps, and
  universal with respect to this property. This time, universal means that given
  another $W$ and maps as above, there is a unique map of vector spaces $V -> W$
  whose composite with each given map $cal(F)(x) -> V$ is the given map
  $cal(F)(x) -> W$.
] <def:inverse-direct-limit>

We shall also write $H^0$ for $invlim$ and $H_0$ for $dirlim$#idx(
  "$H^0$, $H_0$",
), for reasons which I hope will become apparent.

The usual methods may be used to show that these concepts exist and are unique
up to canonical isomorphism. It is helpful to have a concrete model #source(
  252,
)for these concepts. The space $invlim cal(F)$ may be constructed as the
subspace of $product_(x in X) cal(F)(x)$ consisting of elements whose
coordinates are related by the restriction maps whenever the corresponding
elements of the poset (resp. simplices) are comparable.

Dually, the space $dirlim cal(F)$ may be constructed as the quotient of
$plus.o.big_(x in X) cal(F)(x)$ by the subspace generated by elements with all
except two coordinates zero, and the remaining two related by a restriction map.

It is not hard to prove the above universal properties from these constructions.
This is left as a (boring) exercise for the reader. Note that the universal
properties ensure that the spaces $invlim cal(F)$ and $dirlim cal(F)$ come
equipped with a $G$-action, so that they are $k G$-modules.

#example(kind: "Examples")[
  If $X$ is the poset with trivial group action
  $ #pushout-poset $
  (where an arrow denotes an inequality in which the head of the arrow is less
  than the tail) then $dirlim cal(F)$ is the same as the *pushout*#idx(
    "pushout",
  ) of the vector spaces at the vertices. Dually, if $X$ is the poset with
  trivial group action
  $ #pullback-poset $
  then $invlim cal(F)$ is the same as the *pullback*#idx("pullback") of the
  vector spaces at the vertices.
]

#exercise[
  If $0 -> cal(F) -> cal(G) -> cal(G) slash cal(F) -> 0$ is a short exact
  sequence of coefficient systems, show that
  + $0 -> H^0 cal(F) -> H^0 cal(G) -> H^0 (cal(G) slash cal(F))$ and
  + $H_0 cal(F) -> H_0 cal(G) -> H_0 (cal(G) slash cal(F)) -> 0$
  are exact (in other words, $invlim$ is left exact while $dirlim$ is right
  exact; the side of the exactness can be remembered by the direction in which
  the arrow points).
]

If $f : X -> Y$ is a map of $G$-posets, there are various ways of passing from a
$G$-equivariant coefficient system on $X$ to a $G$-equivariant coefficient
system on $Y$ and vice-versa.

#definition[
  If $cal(F)$ is a coefficient system on $X$ and $cal(G)$ is a coefficient
  system on $Y$, we define
  + $f^(-1) cal(G)(x) = cal(G)(f(x))$#idx("$f^(-1) cal(G)$"). For example, if
    $i : X arrow.r.hook Y$ is an inclusion, then $i^(-1) cal(G)$ is the
    *restriction* of $cal(G)$ to $X$.
  + #idx("$f_* cal(F)$")$f_* cal(F)(y) = invlim_(x in f slash y) cal(F)(x)$ (so
    that in particular $f_*$ is left exact). The notations
    $y class("normal", backslash) f$ and $f slash y$ are explained in
    Section~@sec:quillen-lemma.
  + #idx("$f_! cal(F)$")$f_! cal(F)(y)
    = dirlim_(x in y class("normal", backslash) f) cal(F)(x)$ (so that in
    particular $f_!$ is right exact).
] <def:coefficient-system-pullback-pushforward>

#lemma[
  #source(253)If $f : X -> Y$ is a map of $G$-posets, then there are natural
  isomorphisms
  + $Hom_(X, G) (cal(F), f^(-1) cal(G))
    tilde.equiv Hom_(Y, G) (f_! cal(F), cal(G))$
  + $Hom_(X, G) (f^(-1) cal(G), cal(F))
    tilde.equiv Hom_(Y, G) (cal(G), f_* cal(F))$
] <lem:coefficient-system-adjunctions>

#proof[
  + Giving a map $cal(F) -> f^(-1) cal(G)$ is the same as giving a consistent
    set of maps $cal(F)(x) -> cal(G)(f(x))$ for all $x in X$. By the universal
    property of direct limits, this is the same as giving a consistent set of
    maps
    $ dirlim_(x in y class("normal", backslash) f) cal(F)(x) -> cal(G)(y), $
    i.e., a map $f_! cal(F) -> cal(G)$. Note that if $f$ is an inclusion and
    $y = f(x_0)$ then
    $ dirlim_(x in y class("normal", backslash) f) cal(F)(x) = cal(F)(x_0). $
  The proof of (ii) is dual.
]

Note that in particular if we take $cal(F) = f^(-1) cal(G)$ in the above lemma,
the maps corresponding to the identity map under the above natural isomorphisms
are naturally defined maps $f_! f^(-1) cal(G) -> cal(G)$ and
$cal(G) -> f_* f^(-1) cal(G)$.

#example(kind: "Example: Frobenius Reciprocity")[
  #idx("Frobenius", "reciprocity")If we take $Y$ to be the poset consisting of a
  single point in the above lemma, we obtain the following. $G$-equivariant
  coefficient systems on $(p t)$ are the same thing as $k G$-modules. If we
  denote by $f$ the unique map from $X$ to $(p t)$, then
  $f^(-1) (M_((p t))) = kappa_M$, the constant coefficient system on $X$ with
  values in $M$.
]

#proposition(closed: true)[
  If $cal(F)$ is a $G$-equivariant coefficient system on $X$, and $M$ is a
  $k G$-module, then there are natural isomorphisms
  + $Hom_(X, G) (cal(F), kappa_M) tilde.equiv Hom_(k G) (H_0 (cal(F)), M)$
  + $Hom_(X, G) (kappa_M, cal(F)) tilde.equiv Hom_(k G) (M, H^0 (cal(F)))$.
] <prop:coefficient-system-frobenius-reciprocity>

Taking $cal(F) = cal(F)_M$ we obtain a natural map $H_0 (cal(F)_M) -> M$,
corresponding to the inclusion $cal(F)_M arrow.r.hook kappa_M$. We shall be
investigating this map in the section after next.

#definition[
  If $i : X arrow.r.hook Y$ is an inclusion and $cal(F)$ is a $G$-equivariant
  coefficient system on $X$, then $i_! cal(F)$ is the *universal extension*#idx(
    "extension",
    "universal",
  )#idx("universal", "extension") of $cal(F)$ to $Y$.
] <def:universal-extension>

The reason for making this definition is the following characterisation.

#proposition(closed: true)[
  Let $i : X arrow.r.hook Y$ be an inclusion, and $cal(G)$ a coefficient system
  on $Y$. Then the following are equivalent.
  + The natural map $i_! i^(-1) cal(G) -> cal(G)$ is surjective.
  + For each $y in Y$, $y in.not X$, $cal(G)(y)$ is generated by the
    $res_(x, y) cal(G)(x)$ for $x >= y$, $x in X$.
] <prop:generated-by-restriction>

In case these equivalent conditions hold, we say that $cal(G)$ is
*generated*#idx("coefficient system", "generating")#idx(
  "generating",
  "coefficient system",
) by its restriction to $X$. Thus the universal extension $i_! cal(F)$ has the
property that _whenever the restriction of $cal(G)$ to $X$ is isomorphic to
$cal(F)$, and $cal(G)$ is generated by this restriction, then $cal(G)$ is a
quotient of $i_! cal(F)$_.
